    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d296">

                <pb facs="0417" n="409" sameAs="frus:frus1941v04/pg409"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1989/14515: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-08-29T23:00:00+09:00">August 29, 1941—11
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received August 29—3:25 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1348. As it has been difficult to see the Foreign Minister lately I wrote him a
                    letter today conveying the substance of Department’s <pb facs="0418" n="410"
                        xml:id="pg410"/> 527, August 23, 10 [<hi rendition="#italic">11</hi>] a.m.
                    making clear through certain public statements by officers of the United States
                    Government that there is no justification for the allegations and apprehensions
                    entertained by the Japanese press that Japan is being encircled and that aid
                    given Soviet Russia by the United States constitutes a threat to Japan as a part
                    of that encirclement.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
